exports[`reporter output — golden-file snapshots (improvement_plan.md 5.1) > clean-agent > matches the golden sarif output 1`] = `
"{
  "$schema": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/oasis-tcs/sarif-spec/master/Schemata/sarif-schema-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "chaperone",
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone",
          "version": "0.1.0",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CHAP-SEC-001",
              "name": "Plaintext secrets in config",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Plaintext secrets in config"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "API keys, tokens, passwords, and similar credentials stored directly in the config file as literal values, whether under a secret-named key or recognizable from the value itself."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "API keys, tokens, passwords, and similar credentials stored directly in the config file as literal values, whether under a secret-named key or recognizable from the value itself.\\n\\nHow it's detected: A config key whose name looks secret-bearing (\`api_key\`, \`token\`, \`secret\`, \`password\`, \`credential\`, case-insensitive) holds a literal string value rather than an indirect reference (\`\${VAR}\`, \`$VAR\`, \`env:VAR\`). Keys ending in \`auth\`, \`authorization\`, \`bearer\`, \`cookie\`, or \`session\` count only when the value looks like a credential rather than a word. Independently of the key name, a value is flagged when it matches a high-precision pattern: a provider-prefixed token (\`sk-ant-\`, \`sk-proj-\`, \`ghp_\`, \`github_pat_\`, \`xoxb-\`, \`AKIA\`, \`AIza\`, \`glpat-\`, \`npm_\`, \`hf_\`, \`sk_live_\`), a \`Bearer\`/\`Basic\` credential, a PEM private key, or a password in a URL (\`https://user:pass@host\`). No entropy guessing. The literal value is masked (e.g. \`sk-…wxyz\`) before it ever reaches this check or any report — the real value is never printed. Findings include the source line, for YAML and JSON configs alike (JSON is parsed for positions with \`YAML.parseDocument\`).\\n\\nRemediation: Move the value to an environment variable or a secrets manager and reference it indirectly in config.",
                "markdown": "API keys, tokens, passwords, and similar credentials stored directly in the config file as literal values, whether under a secret-named key or recognizable from the value itself.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** A config key whose name looks secret-bearing (\`api_key\`, \`token\`, \`secret\`, \`password\`, \`credential\`, case-insensitive) holds a literal string value rather than an indirect reference (\`\${VAR}\`, \`$VAR\`, \`env:VAR\`). Keys ending in \`auth\`, \`authorization\`, \`bearer\`, \`cookie\`, or \`session\` count only when the value looks like a credential rather than a word. Independently of the key name, a value is flagged when it matches a high-precision pattern: a provider-prefixed token (\`sk-ant-\`, \`sk-proj-\`, \`ghp_\`, \`github_pat_\`, \`xoxb-\`, \`AKIA\`, \`AIza\`, \`glpat-\`, \`npm_\`, \`hf_\`, \`sk_live_\`), a \`Bearer\`/\`Basic\` credential, a PEM private key, or a password in a URL (\`https://user:pass@host\`). No entropy guessing. The literal value is masked (e.g. \`sk-…wxyz\`) before it ever reaches this check or any report — the real value is never printed. Findings include the source line, for YAML and JSON configs alike (JSON is parsed for positions with \`YAML.parseDocument\`).\\n\\n**Remediation:** Move the value to an environment variable or a secrets manager and reference it indirectly in config."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-sec-001--plaintext-secrets-in-config",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "secrets",
                "owasp": "LLM06: Sensitive Information Disclosure",
                "severity": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "secrets"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-SEC-002",
              "name": "Secrets in a git-tracked path",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Secrets in a git-tracked path"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A config file holding a literal secret that sits inside a git repository without being covered by that repo's \`.gitignore\`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A config file holding a literal secret that sits inside a git repository without being covered by that repo's \`.gitignore\`.\\n\\nHow it's detected: An ancestor \`.git\` directory exists above the config file (discovery-detected), the config holds at least one literal secret (see CHAP-SEC-001), and the config's path relative to the repo root doesn't match any pattern in the repo-root or a nested \`.gitignore\` between the git root and the scanned target. Matching uses the \`ignore\` npm package (real gitignore semantics, including \`**\` and nested \`.gitignore\` files); see \`checks/shared/gitignoreMatch.ts\`. Known gap, not solved: \`.git/info/exclude\` and a user's global \`core.excludesFile\` aren't read, and \\"not gitignored\\" isn't the same as \\"actually tracked\\" (see DECISIONS.md).\\n\\nRemediation: Add the config/secret file to .gitignore, and rotate any key that may already have been committed.",
                "markdown": "A config file holding a literal secret that sits inside a git repository without being covered by that repo's \`.gitignore\`.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** An ancestor \`.git\` directory exists above the config file (discovery-detected), the config holds at least one literal secret (see CHAP-SEC-001), and the config's path relative to the repo root doesn't match any pattern in the repo-root or a nested \`.gitignore\` between the git root and the scanned target. Matching uses the \`ignore\` npm package (real gitignore semantics, including \`**\` and nested \`.gitignore\` files); see \`checks/shared/gitignoreMatch.ts\`. Known gap, not solved: \`.git/info/exclude\` and a user's global \`core.excludesFile\` aren't read, and \\"not gitignored\\" isn't the same as \\"actually tracked\\" (see DECISIONS.md).\\n\\n**Remediation:** Add the config/secret file to .gitignore, and rotate any key that may already have been committed."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-sec-002--secrets-in-a-git-tracked-path",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "secrets",
                "owasp": "LLM06: Sensitive Information Disclosure",
                "severity": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "secrets"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-SEC-003",
              "name": "Overly permissive file permissions",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Overly permissive file permissions"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The config file being readable by group or other."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The config file being readable by group or other.\\n\\nHow it's detected: POSIX file mode broader than \`0600\` (i.e. any group/other read bit set). Meaningful on macOS/Linux; not a reliable signal on platforms without POSIX permission bits.\\n\\nRemediation: \`chmod 600\` the config file (and \`chmod 700\` its directory).",
                "markdown": "The config file being readable by group or other.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** POSIX file mode broader than \`0600\` (i.e. any group/other read bit set). Meaningful on macOS/Linux; not a reliable signal on platforms without POSIX permission bits.\\n\\n**Remediation:** \`chmod 600\` the config file (and \`chmod 700\` its directory)."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-sec-003--overly-permissive-file-permissions",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "secrets",
                "owasp": "LLM06: Sensitive Information Disclosure",
                "severity": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "secrets"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-SEC-004",
              "name": "Secrets likely to reach logs",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Secrets likely to reach logs"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Verbose logging that's likely to capture secrets, or a log file itself exposed to other local users."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Verbose logging that's likely to capture secrets, or a log file itself exposed to other local users.\\n\\nHow it's detected: Fires when either: the log level is \`debug\`/\`trace\`/\`verbose\` while the config holds a literal secret, or the log file is readable by group/other. Either reason alone is enough; both are reported together when both hold.\\n\\nRemediation: Raise the log level away from debug/trace, redact secrets before logging, and restrict the log file to owner-only access.",
                "markdown": "Verbose logging that's likely to capture secrets, or a log file itself exposed to other local users.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** Fires when either: the log level is \`debug\`/\`trace\`/\`verbose\` while the config holds a literal secret, or the log file is readable by group/other. Either reason alone is enough; both are reported together when both hold.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Raise the log level away from debug/trace, redact secrets before logging, and restrict the log file to owner-only access."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-sec-004--secrets-likely-to-reach-logs",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "secrets",
                "owasp": "LLM06: Sensitive Information Disclosure",
                "severity": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "secrets"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-SEC-005",
              "name": "Secret already present in existing log content",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Secret already present in existing log content"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A secret-shaped value already written into the log file's existing content — distinct from CHAP-SEC-004/CHAP-OBS-002, which only reason about whether logging config is likely to leak going forward, not whether it already has."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A secret-shaped value already written into the log file's existing content — distinct from CHAP-SEC-004/CHAP-OBS-002, which only reason about whether logging config is likely to leak going forward, not whether it already has.\\n\\nHow it's detected: (Deliberately simple, v1.) Scans up to the last 256 KiB of the log file (bounded — see \`discovery/logContentScanner.ts\`) for \`key=value\`/\`\\"key\\": \\"value\\"\`-shaped substrings where the key looks secret-bearing (the same \`looksLikeSecretKeyName\` heuristic CHAP-SEC-001 uses) and the value is at least 8 characters. Independently, it flags secret-shaped values anywhere in a line using the same high-precision value patterns as CHAP-SEC-001 (provider-prefixed tokens, a logged \`Authorization: Bearer …\` header, a password in a URL); a value already matched by the key=value pass is not reported twice. A generic high-entropy-string scanner was the documented alternative (\`improvement_plan.md\` 2.1); this reuses existing, tested logic instead. Matched values are masked before ever reaching the model — the real value is never retained or printed, same guarantee as config secrets.\\n\\nRemediation: Rotate the leaked credential, purge or redact the log file, and fix the logging behavior that caused it to be written.",
                "markdown": "A secret-shaped value already written into the log file's existing content — distinct from CHAP-SEC-004/CHAP-OBS-002, which only reason about whether logging config is likely to leak going forward, not whether it already has.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** (Deliberately simple, v1.) Scans up to the last 256 KiB of the log file (bounded — see \`discovery/logContentScanner.ts\`) for \`key=value\`/\`\\"key\\": \\"value\\"\`-shaped substrings where the key looks secret-bearing (the same \`looksLikeSecretKeyName\` heuristic CHAP-SEC-001 uses) and the value is at least 8 characters. Independently, it flags secret-shaped values anywhere in a line using the same high-precision value patterns as CHAP-SEC-001 (provider-prefixed tokens, a logged \`Authorization: Bearer …\` header, a password in a URL); a value already matched by the key=value pass is not reported twice. A generic high-entropy-string scanner was the documented alternative (\`improvement_plan.md\` 2.1); this reuses existing, tested logic instead. Matched values are masked before ever reaching the model — the real value is never retained or printed, same guarantee as config secrets.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Rotate the leaked credential, purge or redact the log file, and fix the logging behavior that caused it to be written."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-sec-005--secret-already-present-in-existing-log-content",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "secrets",
                "owasp": "LLM06: Sensitive Information Disclosure",
                "severity": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "secrets"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-SEC-006",
              "name": "Sidecar secret file exposed",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Sidecar secret file exposed"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A sidecar secret file discovered alongside the main config (\`.env\`, \`.env.*\` except example/sample/template files, \`.envrc\`, \`secrets.yaml/.yml/.json\`, \`credentials.json\`, \`service-account*.json\`, \`.npmrc\`, \`.pypirc\`, \`.netrc\`, or a private key: \`id_rsa\`/\`id_ecdsa\`/\`id_ed25519\`, \`*.pem\`/\`*.key\` holding a PEM private key), holding a literal secret that's either git-tracked and not gitignored, or readable by group/other."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A sidecar secret file discovered alongside the main config (\`.env\`, \`.env.*\` except example/sample/template files, \`.envrc\`, \`secrets.yaml/.yml/.json\`, \`credentials.json\`, \`service-account*.json\`, \`.npmrc\`, \`.pypirc\`, \`.netrc\`, or a private key: \`id_rsa\`/\`id_ecdsa\`/\`id_ed25519\`, \`*.pem\`/\`*.key\` holding a PEM private key), holding a literal secret that's either git-tracked and not gitignored, or readable by group/other.\\n\\nHow it's detected: Applies the exact same two checks CHAP-SEC-002/CHAP-SEC-003 apply to the main config file, to each discovered sidecar file instead: the file holds at least one literal (non-env-reference) secret field, recognized by key name or by value pattern exactly as in CHAP-SEC-001 (masked the same way \`config.yaml\` is — see \`discovery/configParser.ts\`/\`discovery/sidecarSecrets.ts\`), and either its path relative to an ancestor git root isn't covered by the repo's \`.gitignore\`, or its POSIX mode is broader than \`0600\`. Both reasons are reported together in one finding when both hold.\\n\\nRemediation: Add the file to .gitignore, rotate any key that may already have been committed, and restrict it to owner-only access (chmod 600).",
                "markdown": "A sidecar secret file discovered alongside the main config (\`.env\`, \`.env.*\` except example/sample/template files, \`.envrc\`, \`secrets.yaml/.yml/.json\`, \`credentials.json\`, \`service-account*.json\`, \`.npmrc\`, \`.pypirc\`, \`.netrc\`, or a private key: \`id_rsa\`/\`id_ecdsa\`/\`id_ed25519\`, \`*.pem\`/\`*.key\` holding a PEM private key), holding a literal secret that's either git-tracked and not gitignored, or readable by group/other.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** Applies the exact same two checks CHAP-SEC-002/CHAP-SEC-003 apply to the main config file, to each discovered sidecar file instead: the file holds at least one literal (non-env-reference) secret field, recognized by key name or by value pattern exactly as in CHAP-SEC-001 (masked the same way \`config.yaml\` is — see \`discovery/configParser.ts\`/\`discovery/sidecarSecrets.ts\`), and either its path relative to an ancestor git root isn't covered by the repo's \`.gitignore\`, or its POSIX mode is broader than \`0600\`. Both reasons are reported together in one finding when both hold.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Add the file to .gitignore, rotate any key that may already have been committed, and restrict it to owner-only access (chmod 600)."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-sec-006--sidecar-secret-file-exposed",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "secrets",
                "owasp": "LLM06: Sensitive Information Disclosure",
                "severity": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "secrets"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-SEC-007",
              "name": "Config references an environment variable that isn't set",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Config references an environment variable that isn't set"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A config field using a bare \`\${VAR}\`/\`$VAR\`/\`env:VAR\` reference where \`VAR\` isn't set (or is empty) in Chaperone's own process environment at scan time."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A config field using a bare \`\${VAR}\`/\`$VAR\`/\`env:VAR\` reference where \`VAR\` isn't set (or is empty) in Chaperone's own process environment at scan time.\\n\\nHow it's detected: (Explicitly advisory/low-confidence.) Chaperone runs as a separate process from the agent and may not share its real environment — e.g. the agent could be launched via \`systemd\`/\`launchd\` with its own \`EnvironmentFile\` Chaperone never sees. The finding message states this caveat directly, not just here. Only bare references are checked; a reference with a \`:-\`/\`:=\`/\`:?\`/\`:+\` fallback/default resolves to something even when the variable itself is unset, so it's silently skipped rather than risk a false positive. Reports the source line number for YAML and JSON configs (see CHAP-SEC-001).\\n\\nRemediation: Confirm the variable is actually set in the environment the agent runs under; an unresolved reference can mean the agent starts with an empty or broken credential.",
                "markdown": "A config field using a bare \`\${VAR}\`/\`$VAR\`/\`env:VAR\` reference where \`VAR\` isn't set (or is empty) in Chaperone's own process environment at scan time.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** (Explicitly advisory/low-confidence.) Chaperone runs as a separate process from the agent and may not share its real environment — e.g. the agent could be launched via \`systemd\`/\`launchd\` with its own \`EnvironmentFile\` Chaperone never sees. The finding message states this caveat directly, not just here. Only bare references are checked; a reference with a \`:-\`/\`:=\`/\`:?\`/\`:+\` fallback/default resolves to something even when the variable itself is unset, so it's silently skipped rather than risk a false positive. Reports the source line number for YAML and JSON configs (see CHAP-SEC-001).\\n\\n**Remediation:** Confirm the variable is actually set in the environment the agent runs under; an unresolved reference can mean the agent starts with an empty or broken credential."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-sec-007--config-references-an-environment-variable-that-isnt-set",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "secrets",
                "owasp": "LLM06: Sensitive Information Disclosure",
                "severity": "low",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "secrets"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-SEC-008",
              "name": "Agent files writable by other users",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Agent files writable by other users"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The config file, install directory, skills directory, any skill directory, or the memory directory being writable by users other than the owner."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The config file, install directory, skills directory, any skill directory, or the memory directory being writable by users other than the owner.\\n\\nHow it's detected: POSIX mode with a group or other write bit (\`0o022\`) set on any of those paths. A writable skills directory lets any local user or process plant code that the agent runs with its own privileges (a local privilege escalation); writable memory lets them inject persistent instructions. Meaningful on macOS/Linux only: on Windows, Node's reported mode doesn't reflect other users' access, so no finding is reported there.\\n\\nRemediation: Remove group/other write access, e.g. \`chmod go-w <path>\` (\`chmod -R go-w\` for a skills directory), and make sure the agent runs as a dedicated user that owns its files.",
                "markdown": "The config file, install directory, skills directory, any skill directory, or the memory directory being writable by users other than the owner.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** POSIX mode with a group or other write bit (\`0o022\`) set on any of those paths. A writable skills directory lets any local user or process plant code that the agent runs with its own privileges (a local privilege escalation); writable memory lets them inject persistent instructions. Meaningful on macOS/Linux only: on Windows, Node's reported mode doesn't reflect other users' access, so no finding is reported there.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Remove group/other write access, e.g. \`chmod go-w <path>\` (\`chmod -R go-w\` for a skills directory), and make sure the agent runs as a dedicated user that owns its files."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-sec-008--agent-files-writable-by-other-users",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "secrets",
                "owasp": "LLM05: Supply Chain",
                "severity": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "secrets"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-SEC-009",
              "name": "Secret files readable by Claude Code",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Secret files readable by Claude Code"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Claude Code settings with no deny rule keeping its file tools away from common secret files: \`.env\` files in the project and SSH keys in \`~/.ssh\`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Claude Code settings with no deny rule keeping its file tools away from common secret files: \`.env\` files in the project and SSH keys in \`~/.ssh\`.\\n\\nHow it's detected: Across every settings file read, no \`permissions.deny\` rule for \`Read\` (bare, or with a path containing \`.env\`, or a recursive \`**\` pattern) covers \`.env\` files, or none (containing \`.ssh\`, or \`~/**\`/\`//**\`) covers \`~/.ssh\`. Only reported when at least one settings file exists. Path coverage is approximate: it checks for the file name in the rule, not full glob matching.\\n\\nRemediation: Add \`Read\` deny rules for secret files, e.g. \`\\"deny\\": [\\"Read(./.env)\\", \\"Read(./.env.*)\\", \\"Read(~/.ssh/**)\\"]\`. Claude Code reads files in the working directory without asking, and a \`.claudeignore\` file has no effect.",
                "markdown": "Claude Code settings with no deny rule keeping its file tools away from common secret files: \`.env\` files in the project and SSH keys in \`~/.ssh\`.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** Across every settings file read, no \`permissions.deny\` rule for \`Read\` (bare, or with a path containing \`.env\`, or a recursive \`**\` pattern) covers \`.env\` files, or none (containing \`.ssh\`, or \`~/**\`/\`//**\`) covers \`~/.ssh\`. Only reported when at least one settings file exists. Path coverage is approximate: it checks for the file name in the rule, not full glob matching.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Add \`Read\` deny rules for secret files, e.g. \`\\"deny\\": [\\"Read(./.env)\\", \\"Read(./.env.*)\\", \\"Read(~/.ssh/**)\\"]\`. Claude Code reads files in the working directory without asking, and a \`.claudeignore\` file has no effect."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-sec-009--secret-files-readable-by-claude-code",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "secrets",
                "owasp": "LLM06: Sensitive Information Disclosure",
                "severity": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "secrets"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-AGY-001",
              "name": "Unrestricted shell execution",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unrestricted shell execution"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Skills/plugins that can run arbitrary shell commands."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Skills/plugins that can run arbitrary shell commands.\\n\\nHow it's detected: A skill's source contains a shell/exec/spawn capability: \`child_process\` (\`exec\`/\`execFile\`/\`spawn\`/\`fork\` and their sync forms), \`execa\`, \`shelljs.exec\`, \`zx\` \`$\`, \`cross-spawn\`, \`node-pty\`, \`Bun.spawn\`, or \`new Deno.Command\`. One level of aliasing (\`const run = cp['exec']\`), nested and template-literal member access, and inline \`require(...)\` are resolved. A command allowlist isn't detected, so any detected shell capability is treated as unrestricted. If the skill's manifest declares \`confirmationRequired: true\`, the finding is downgraded to High: the gate is self-declared and can't be verified statically.\\n\\nRemediation: Constrain the skill to an explicit command allowlist, require confirmation for shell actions, or sandbox its execution.",
                "markdown": "Skills/plugins that can run arbitrary shell commands.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** A skill's source contains a shell/exec/spawn capability: \`child_process\` (\`exec\`/\`execFile\`/\`spawn\`/\`fork\` and their sync forms), \`execa\`, \`shelljs.exec\`, \`zx\` \`$\`, \`cross-spawn\`, \`node-pty\`, \`Bun.spawn\`, or \`new Deno.Command\`. One level of aliasing (\`const run = cp['exec']\`), nested and template-literal member access, and inline \`require(...)\` are resolved. A command allowlist isn't detected, so any detected shell capability is treated as unrestricted. If the skill's manifest declares \`confirmationRequired: true\`, the finding is downgraded to High: the gate is self-declared and can't be verified statically.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Constrain the skill to an explicit command allowlist, require confirmation for shell actions, or sandbox its execution."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-agy-001--unrestricted-shell-execution",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "agency",
                "owasp": "LLM08: Excessive Agency",
                "severity": "critical",
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "agency"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-AGY-002",
              "name": "Unrestricted filesystem access",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unrestricted filesystem access"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Skills that write/delete files with no scoping to a fixed workspace directory."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Skills that write/delete files with no scoping to a fixed workspace directory.\\n\\nHow it's detected: The skill writes files (\`fs\`/\`fs/promises\`/\`fs-extra\` writes, renames, copies, deletes, \`mkdir\`/\`chmod\`/\`createWriteStream\`, including \`fs.promises.*\`; or any call into \`rimraf\`/\`del\`) and at least one write's path isn't scoped. Scoping is decided per write call: a path is scoped when it is the skill's own directory (\`__dirname\`, \`import.meta.dirname\`/\`url\`, or a variable derived from them), a fixed literal path other than a filesystem root, a variable holding one of those, or \`path.join\`/\`path.resolve\` whose first argument is one of those. A caller-supplied path is unscoped, and one unscoped write anywhere in the skill is enough. Path traversal through a joined segment (\`../\`) isn't modeled. Python skills still use a file-level pattern (\`os.path.dirname(__file__)\`, a \`WORKSPACE\`/\`SANDBOX\`-named variable).\\n\\nRemediation: Scope the skill's file access to a dedicated workspace directory and deny path traversal outside it.",
                "markdown": "Skills that write/delete files with no scoping to a fixed workspace directory.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** The skill writes files (\`fs\`/\`fs/promises\`/\`fs-extra\` writes, renames, copies, deletes, \`mkdir\`/\`chmod\`/\`createWriteStream\`, including \`fs.promises.*\`; or any call into \`rimraf\`/\`del\`) and at least one write's path isn't scoped. Scoping is decided per write call: a path is scoped when it is the skill's own directory (\`__dirname\`, \`import.meta.dirname\`/\`url\`, or a variable derived from them), a fixed literal path other than a filesystem root, a variable holding one of those, or \`path.join\`/\`path.resolve\` whose first argument is one of those. A caller-supplied path is unscoped, and one unscoped write anywhere in the skill is enough. Path traversal through a joined segment (\`../\`) isn't modeled. Python skills still use a file-level pattern (\`os.path.dirname(__file__)\`, a \`WORKSPACE\`/\`SANDBOX\`-named variable).\\n\\n**Remediation:** Scope the skill's file access to a dedicated workspace directory and deny path traversal outside it."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-agy-002--unrestricted-filesystem-access",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "agency",
                "owasp": "LLM08: Excessive Agency",
                "severity": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "agency"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-AGY-003",
              "name": "Destructive action without confirmation",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Destructive action without confirmation"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Skills that can delete data, send messages, spend money, or otherwise act irreversibly with no human-in-the-loop gate."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Skills that can delete data, send messages, spend money, or otherwise act irreversibly with no human-in-the-loop gate.\\n\\nHow it's detected: The skill's code names a destructive action (\`delete\`, \`send\`, \`transfer\`, \`purchase\`, \`deploy\`, \`remove\`, \`pay\` as a whole word segment, so \`deleteFile\` and \`delete_file\` count): a JS/TS function or method name, or a call to one (bare \`.send()\`/\`.delete()\`/\`.remove()\` member calls on objects are ignored), or a module-level Python \`def\` name. Comments and strings never count, and its manifest does not declare \`confirmationRequired: true\` (checked at the manifest's top level or nested under \`capabilities\`) — an invented-but-documented convention, no real manifest schema exists for these example agents (see DECISIONS.md).\\n\\nRemediation: Require explicit confirmation before this action runs (or add a dry-run mode).",
                "markdown": "Skills that can delete data, send messages, spend money, or otherwise act irreversibly with no human-in-the-loop gate.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** The skill's code names a destructive action (\`delete\`, \`send\`, \`transfer\`, \`purchase\`, \`deploy\`, \`remove\`, \`pay\` as a whole word segment, so \`deleteFile\` and \`delete_file\` count): a JS/TS function or method name, or a call to one (bare \`.send()\`/\`.delete()\`/\`.remove()\` member calls on objects are ignored), or a module-level Python \`def\` name. Comments and strings never count, and its manifest does not declare \`confirmationRequired: true\` (checked at the manifest's top level or nested under \`capabilities\`) — an invented-but-documented convention, no real manifest schema exists for these example agents (see DECISIONS.md).\\n\\n**Remediation:** Require explicit confirmation before this action runs (or add a dry-run mode)."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-agy-003--destructive-action-without-confirmation",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "agency",
                "owasp": "LLM08: Excessive Agency",
                "severity": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "agency"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-AGY-004",
              "name": "Broad network egress from a skill",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Broad network egress from a skill"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Skills permitted to call arbitrary external endpoints."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Skills permitted to call arbitrary external endpoints.\\n\\nHow it's detected: The skill's source shows network capability (global \`fetch\`/\`WebSocket\`/\`XMLHttpRequest\`/\`EventSource\`, or any call into \`http\`/\`https\`/\`http2\`/\`net\`/\`tls\`/\`dgram\`, \`axios\`, \`node-fetch\`, \`undici\`, \`got\`, \`ky\`, \`superagent\`, \`ws\`) and its manifest declares no non-empty \`domainAllowlist\` array (same manifest-convention caveat as CHAP-AGY-003).\\n\\nRemediation: Allowlist the specific destination domain(s) the skill needs and log outbound calls.",
                "markdown": "Skills permitted to call arbitrary external endpoints.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** The skill's source shows network capability (global \`fetch\`/\`WebSocket\`/\`XMLHttpRequest\`/\`EventSource\`, or any call into \`http\`/\`https\`/\`http2\`/\`net\`/\`tls\`/\`dgram\`, \`axios\`, \`node-fetch\`, \`undici\`, \`got\`, \`ky\`, \`superagent\`, \`ws\`) and its manifest declares no non-empty \`domainAllowlist\` array (same manifest-convention caveat as CHAP-AGY-003).\\n\\n**Remediation:** Allowlist the specific destination domain(s) the skill needs and log outbound calls."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-agy-004--broad-network-egress-from-a-skill",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "agency",
                "owasp": "LLM08: Excessive Agency / LLM06",
                "severity": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "agency"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-AGY-005",
              "name": "Container launched with host-level privileges",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Container launched with host-level privileges"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A container the agent or one of its tools runs in, configured so code inside it can act on the host: an MCP server launched with \`docker run\`/\`podman run\`, or a \`docker-compose.yml\`/\`compose.yaml\` service in the install directory."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A container the agent or one of its tools runs in, configured so code inside it can act on the host: an MCP server launched with \`docker run\`/\`podman run\`, or a \`docker-compose.yml\`/\`compose.yaml\` service in the install directory.\\n\\nHow it's detected: \`--privileged\` / \`privileged: true\`; \`--cap-add\` of \`ALL\` or \`SYS_ADMIN\`; host PID, network, IPC, or user namespace (\`--pid=host\`, \`network_mode: host\`, ...); or a bind mount whose source is \`/\`, \`/var/run/docker.sock\`, \`~\`, or \`$HOME\`. Any one of these makes the container no sandbox: the Docker socket alone is root on the host.\\n\\nRemediation: Drop the host-level options: no --privileged, no --cap-add=ALL/SYS_ADMIN, no host PID/network/IPC namespaces, and no bind mounts of /, the Docker socket, or the home directory. Mount only the specific directories the tool needs, read-only where possible.",
                "markdown": "A container the agent or one of its tools runs in, configured so code inside it can act on the host: an MCP server launched with \`docker run\`/\`podman run\`, or a \`docker-compose.yml\`/\`compose.yaml\` service in the install directory.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** \`--privileged\` / \`privileged: true\`; \`--cap-add\` of \`ALL\` or \`SYS_ADMIN\`; host PID, network, IPC, or user namespace (\`--pid=host\`, \`network_mode: host\`, ...); or a bind mount whose source is \`/\`, \`/var/run/docker.sock\`, \`~\`, or \`$HOME\`. Any one of these makes the container no sandbox: the Docker socket alone is root on the host.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Drop the host-level options: no --privileged, no --cap-add=ALL/SYS_ADMIN, no host PID/network/IPC namespaces, and no bind mounts of /, the Docker socket, or the home directory. Mount only the specific directories the tool needs, read-only where possible."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-agy-005--container-launched-with-host-level-privileges",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "agency",
                "owasp": "LLM08: Excessive Agency",
                "severity": "critical",
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "agency"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-AGY-006",
              "name": "Claude Code runs every tool without asking",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Claude Code runs every tool without asking"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Claude Code settings that start every session in \`bypassPermissions\` mode, where every tool call, including any shell command, runs without a permission prompt."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Claude Code settings that start every session in \`bypassPermissions\` mode, where every tool call, including any shell command, runs without a permission prompt.\\n\\nHow it's detected: \`permissions.defaultMode\` is \`\\"bypassPermissions\\"\`. In user settings this applies to every project: critical. Current Claude Code (v2.1.257+) ignores the value in project and local settings, so there it is reported as low (it still applied on older versions, and it signals intent). \`skipDangerousModePermissionPrompt: true\` is mentioned when set.\\n\\nRemediation: Remove \`permissions.defaultMode: \\"bypassPermissions\\"\` and pre-approve only the specific commands the project needs with \`permissions.allow\` rules. To stop anyone entering the mode, set \`permissions.disableBypassPermissionsMode\`.",
                "markdown": "Claude Code settings that start every session in \`bypassPermissions\` mode, where every tool call, including any shell command, runs without a permission prompt.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** \`permissions.defaultMode\` is \`\\"bypassPermissions\\"\`. In user settings this applies to every project: critical. Current Claude Code (v2.1.257+) ignores the value in project and local settings, so there it is reported as low (it still applied on older versions, and it signals intent). \`skipDangerousModePermissionPrompt: true\` is mentioned when set.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Remove \`permissions.defaultMode: \\"bypassPermissions\\"\` and pre-approve only the specific commands the project needs with \`permissions.allow\` rules. To stop anyone entering the mode, set \`permissions.disableBypassPermissionsMode\`."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-agy-006--claude-code-runs-every-tool-without-asking",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "agency",
                "owasp": "LLM08: Excessive Agency",
                "severity": "critical",
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "agency"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-AGY-007",
              "name": "Shell commands pre-approved too broadly",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Shell commands pre-approved too broadly"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Claude Code \`permissions.allow\` rules that let it run any shell command, or any invocation of an interpreter, downloader, or destructive command, without asking."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Claude Code \`permissions.allow\` rules that let it run any shell command, or any invocation of an interpreter, downloader, or destructive command, without asking.\\n\\nHow it's detected: A bare \`Bash\`/\`PowerShell\` rule, or one whose pattern is only a wildcard (\`Bash(*)\`, \`Bash(:*)\`), is critical. A rule whose command is an interpreter or one that runs, fetches, deletes, or escalates (\`sh\`, \`python\`, \`node\`, \`npx\`, \`curl\`, \`wget\`, \`rm\`, \`sudo\`, \`xargs\`, ...), with a trailing wildcard, is high: \`Bash(python:*)\` approves \`python -c \\"<anything>\\"\`.\\n\\nRemediation: Replace the rule with the specific commands the project needs (e.g. \`Bash(npm run test *)\`), and leave interpreters, downloads, deletes, and privilege escalation to a prompt.",
                "markdown": "Claude Code \`permissions.allow\` rules that let it run any shell command, or any invocation of an interpreter, downloader, or destructive command, without asking.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** A bare \`Bash\`/\`PowerShell\` rule, or one whose pattern is only a wildcard (\`Bash(*)\`, \`Bash(:*)\`), is critical. A rule whose command is an interpreter or one that runs, fetches, deletes, or escalates (\`sh\`, \`python\`, \`node\`, \`npx\`, \`curl\`, \`wget\`, \`rm\`, \`sudo\`, \`xargs\`, ...), with a trailing wildcard, is high: \`Bash(python:*)\` approves \`python -c \\"<anything>\\"\`.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Replace the rule with the specific commands the project needs (e.g. \`Bash(npm run test *)\`), and leave interpreters, downloads, deletes, and privilege escalation to a prompt."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-agy-007--shell-commands-pre-approved-too-broadly",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "agency",
                "owasp": "LLM08: Excessive Agency",
                "severity": "critical",
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "agency"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-AGY-008",
              "name": "Web fetches pre-approved for every domain",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Web fetches pre-approved for every domain"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Claude Code \`permissions.allow\` rule that lets it fetch any URL without asking: a channel for both data exfiltration and prompt injection from attacker-controlled pages."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A Claude Code \`permissions.allow\` rule that lets it fetch any URL without asking: a channel for both data exfiltration and prompt injection from attacker-controlled pages.\\n\\nHow it's detected: A bare \`WebFetch\` allow rule, or \`WebFetch(domain:*)\`. A domain-scoped rule (\`WebFetch(domain:example.com)\`, \`WebFetch(domain:*.example.com)\`) is fine.\\n\\nRemediation: Allow only the domains the project needs, e.g. \`WebFetch(domain:docs.example.com)\`. Fetched pages are untrusted input, so each new domain is a new prompt-injection source.",
                "markdown": "A Claude Code \`permissions.allow\` rule that lets it fetch any URL without asking: a channel for both data exfiltration and prompt injection from attacker-controlled pages.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** A bare \`WebFetch\` allow rule, or \`WebFetch(domain:*)\`. A domain-scoped rule (\`WebFetch(domain:example.com)\`, \`WebFetch(domain:*.example.com)\`) is fine.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Allow only the domains the project needs, e.g. \`WebFetch(domain:docs.example.com)\`. Fetched pages are untrusted input, so each new domain is a new prompt-injection source."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-agy-008--web-fetches-pre-approved-for-every-domain",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "agency",
                "owasp": "LLM08: Excessive Agency",
                "severity": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "agency"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-SUP-001",
              "name": "Skill from an unverified source",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Skill from an unverified source"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Skills installed from an unpinned ref or with no verifiable provenance."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Skills installed from an unpinned ref or with no verifiable provenance.\\n\\nHow it's detected: The skill's manifest doesn't confirm a pinned version/ref (\`pinnedRef !== true\` — covers both an explicitly unpinned version like \`\\"latest\\"\` and a manifest with no version/ref info at all).\\n\\nRemediation: Pin the skill to an explicit version or commit, prefer reviewed sources, and verify the author.",
                "markdown": "Skills installed from an unpinned ref or with no verifiable provenance.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** The skill's manifest doesn't confirm a pinned version/ref (\`pinnedRef !== true\` — covers both an explicitly unpinned version like \`\\"latest\\"\` and a manifest with no version/ref info at all).\\n\\n**Remediation:** Pin the skill to an explicit version or commit, prefer reviewed sources, and verify the author."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-sup-001--skill-from-an-unverified-source",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "supply-chain",
                "owasp": "LLM05: Supply Chain",
                "severity": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "supply-chain"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-SUP-002",
              "name": "No integrity verification for skill dependencies",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "No integrity verification for skill dependencies"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Skill dependencies installed with no lockfile."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Skill dependencies installed with no lockfile.\\n\\nHow it's detected: The skill declares at least one runtime dependency with no lockfile alongside it. npm: a \`package.json\` \`dependencies\` entry and no \`package-lock.json\`/\`yarn.lock\`/\`pnpm-lock.yaml\`. Python (when there is no \`package.json\`): a \`requirements.txt\` or \`pyproject.toml\` dependency and no \`poetry.lock\`/\`uv.lock\`/\`Pipfile.lock\`/\`pdm.lock\`; a \`requirements.txt\` whose every entry has a \`--hash=\` counts as its own lockfile. A manifest with no dependencies has nothing to install, so it is not flagged.\\n\\nRemediation: Commit a lockfile alongside the manifest and enable integrity checks.",
                "markdown": "Skill dependencies installed with no lockfile.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** The skill declares at least one runtime dependency with no lockfile alongside it. npm: a \`package.json\` \`dependencies\` entry and no \`package-lock.json\`/\`yarn.lock\`/\`pnpm-lock.yaml\`. Python (when there is no \`package.json\`): a \`requirements.txt\` or \`pyproject.toml\` dependency and no \`poetry.lock\`/\`uv.lock\`/\`Pipfile.lock\`/\`pdm.lock\`; a \`requirements.txt\` whose every entry has a \`--hash=\` counts as its own lockfile. A manifest with no dependencies has nothing to install, so it is not flagged.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Commit a lockfile alongside the manifest and enable integrity checks."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-sup-002--no-integrity-verification-for-skill-dependencies",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "supply-chain",
                "owasp": "LLM05: Supply Chain",
                "severity": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "supply-chain"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-SUP-003",
              "name": "Known-vulnerable dependency",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Known-vulnerable dependency"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A skill dependency, direct or transitive, whose version matches a known vulnerability in a bundled, offline OSV snapshot (or an OSV export passed with \`--vuln-db\`)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A skill dependency, direct or transitive, whose version matches a known vulnerability in a bundled, offline OSV snapshot (or an OSV export passed with \`--vuln-db\`).\\n\\nHow it's detected: When the skill has a \`package-lock.json\`, \`npm-shrinkwrap.json\`, \`yarn.lock\`, or \`pnpm-lock.yaml\`, every resolved package version in it, direct and transitive, is matched exactly. Without a lockfile, each \`package.json\` specifier's first X.Y.Z token stands in for the version, and the message says so; a specifier with no such token (\`\\"latest\\"\`, \`\\"*\\"\`, a git URL) is skipped. The bundled snapshot (\`shared/vulnDb.ts\`) covers about 80 packages agent skills commonly use and is refreshed out-of-band with \`npm run refresh:vulndb\`, never during a scan. Its date is shown in the report header. npm dependencies only.\\n\\nRemediation: Upgrade the dependency to a patched version (or remove it if unused). Run \`npm audit\` for a live check beyond the offline snapshot, or pass a full OSV export with \`--vuln-db\`.",
                "markdown": "A skill dependency, direct or transitive, whose version matches a known vulnerability in a bundled, offline OSV snapshot (or an OSV export passed with \`--vuln-db\`).\\n\\n**How it's detected:** When the skill has a \`package-lock.json\`, \`npm-shrinkwrap.json\`, \`yarn.lock\`, or \`pnpm-lock.yaml\`, every resolved package version in it, direct and transitive, is matched exactly. Without a lockfile, each \`package.json\` specifier's first X.Y.Z token stands in for the version, and the message says so; a specifier with no such token (\`\\"latest\\"\`, \`\\"*\\"\`, a git URL) is skipped. The bundled snapshot (\`shared/vulnDb.ts\`) covers about 80 packages agent skills commonly use and is refreshed out-of-band with \`npm run refresh:vulndb\`, never during a scan. Its date is shown in the report header. npm dependencies only.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Upgrade the dependency to a patched version (or remove it if unused). Run \`npm audit\` for a live check beyond the offline snapshot, or pass a full OSV export with \`--vuln-db\`."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-sup-003--known-vulnerable-dependency",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "supply-chain",
                "owasp": "LLM05: Supply Chain",
                "severity": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "supply-chain"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-SUP-004",
              "name": "Dangerous install pattern",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Dangerous install pattern"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Skill install scripts/docs that pipe a remote script into a shell, use \`sudo\`, or bootstrap a system package manager."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Skill install scripts/docs that pipe a remote script into a shell, use \`sudo\`, or bootstrap a system package manager.\\n\\nHow it's detected: A download executed by a shell: \`curl\`/\`wget\` piped into \`sh\`/\`bash\`/\`zsh\` (including \`| sudo bash\` and \`| bash -s\`), \`bash <(curl …)\`, PowerShell \`iwr\`/\`irm … | iex\`, or \`base64 -d … | sh\`; plus \`sudo\`. Searched in \`package.json\`'s \`preinstall\`/\`install\`/\`postinstall\`/\`prepare\` scripts (\`prepare\` runs on a git install), every \`*.sh\` file anywhere in the skill, and any top-level \`README*\`. \`apt-get install\`/\`brew install\` count only in install scripts and \`*.sh\` files, since a README recommending a package manager is normal.\\n\\nRemediation: Review the install script by hand; prefer a vetted, minimal setup with no piped-shell or sudo steps.",
                "markdown": "Skill install scripts/docs that pipe a remote script into a shell, use \`sudo\`, or bootstrap a system package manager.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** A download executed by a shell: \`curl\`/\`wget\` piped into \`sh\`/\`bash\`/\`zsh\` (including \`| sudo bash\` and \`| bash -s\`), \`bash <(curl …)\`, PowerShell \`iwr\`/\`irm … | iex\`, or \`base64 -d … | sh\`; plus \`sudo\`. Searched in \`package.json\`'s \`preinstall\`/\`install\`/\`postinstall\`/\`prepare\` scripts (\`prepare\` runs on a git install), every \`*.sh\` file anywhere in the skill, and any top-level \`README*\`. \`apt-get install\`/\`brew install\` count only in install scripts and \`*.sh\` files, since a README recommending a package manager is normal.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Review the install script by hand; prefer a vetted, minimal setup with no piped-shell or sudo steps."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-sup-004--dangerous-install-pattern",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "supply-chain",
                "owasp": "LLM05: Supply Chain",
                "severity": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "supply-chain"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-SUP-005",
              "name": "Obfuscated or dynamically-evaluated code",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Obfuscated or dynamically-evaluated code"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A skill using eval(), the Function constructor, or a decode-then-execute chain (e.g. eval(atob(payload))) to run dynamically-constructed code."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A skill using eval(), the Function constructor, or a decode-then-execute chain (e.g. eval(atob(payload))) to run dynamically-constructed code.\\n\\nHow it's detected: Any call to the \`eval\`/\`Function\` globals in a skill's source: bare, \`new Function(...)\`, indirect (\`(0, eval)(...)\`), through a global object (\`globalThis.eval\`, \`window.eval\`, \`self['eval']\`), or through a local alias. Also \`vm\` code execution (\`runInNewContext\`, \`runInThisContext\`, \`new vm.Script\`, \`compileFunction\`), \`require\`/\`import()\` of a non-literal module name, \`setTimeout\`/\`setInterval\` with a string, and \`module._compile\`. Files over 256 KB (typically minified bundles) aren't parsed; a pattern pre-pass looks for \`eval(\`, \`Function(\`, \`atob(\`, or \`child_process\` instead, and the report's Skipped section says which matched. Flagged unconditionally regardless of what's being evaluated — a real backdoor and a benign use are equally invisible to static review once code is constructed/evaluated at runtime, so there's no confident way to distinguish them from source alone.\\n\\nRemediation: Avoid dynamic code evaluation entirely. If genuinely needed, review the exact string being evaluated by hand and vendor/pin it rather than constructing or fetching it at runtime.",
                "markdown": "A skill using eval(), the Function constructor, or a decode-then-execute chain (e.g. eval(atob(payload))) to run dynamically-constructed code.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** Any call to the \`eval\`/\`Function\` globals in a skill's source: bare, \`new Function(...)\`, indirect (\`(0, eval)(...)\`), through a global object (\`globalThis.eval\`, \`window.eval\`, \`self['eval']\`), or through a local alias. Also \`vm\` code execution (\`runInNewContext\`, \`runInThisContext\`, \`new vm.Script\`, \`compileFunction\`), \`require\`/\`import()\` of a non-literal module name, \`setTimeout\`/\`setInterval\` with a string, and \`module._compile\`. Files over 256 KB (typically minified bundles) aren't parsed; a pattern pre-pass looks for \`eval(\`, \`Function(\`, \`atob(\`, or \`child_process\` instead, and the report's Skipped section says which matched. Flagged unconditionally regardless of what's being evaluated — a real backdoor and a benign use are equally invisible to static review once code is constructed/evaluated at runtime, so there's no confident way to distinguish them from source alone.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Avoid dynamic code evaluation entirely. If genuinely needed, review the exact string being evaluated by hand and vendor/pin it rather than constructing or fetching it at runtime."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-sup-005--obfuscated-or-dynamically-evaluated-code",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "supply-chain",
                "owasp": "LLM05: Supply Chain",
                "severity": "critical",
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "supply-chain"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-SUP-006",
              "name": "Typosquat-risk dependency name",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Typosquat-risk dependency name"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A skill dependency whose name is suspiciously close (small edit distance) to a well-known popular package name (e.g. 'reqeust' vs 'request') — a common typosquatting technique."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A skill dependency whose name is suspiciously close (small edit distance) to a well-known popular package name (e.g. 'reqeust' vs 'request') — a common typosquatting technique.\\n\\nHow it's detected: The dependency name isn't itself a well-known package, is at least 4 characters, and has a Levenshtein (edit) distance of 1-2 from a well-known package name in a small bundled reference list (no live registry lookup — Chaperone makes no outbound network calls). Not exhaustive: a name not close to anything on that list is never flagged.\\n\\nRemediation: Double check the exact spelling against the real package on the registry before installing, and remove the dependency if it was added by mistake.",
                "markdown": "A skill dependency whose name is suspiciously close (small edit distance) to a well-known popular package name (e.g. 'reqeust' vs 'request') — a common typosquatting technique.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** The dependency name isn't itself a well-known package, is at least 4 characters, and has a Levenshtein (edit) distance of 1-2 from a well-known package name in a small bundled reference list (no live registry lookup — Chaperone makes no outbound network calls). Not exhaustive: a name not close to anything on that list is never flagged.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Double check the exact spelling against the real package on the registry before installing, and remove the dependency if it was added by mistake."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-sup-006--typosquat-risk-dependency-name",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "supply-chain",
                "owasp": "LLM05: Supply Chain",
                "severity": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "supply-chain"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-SUP-007",
              "name": "Known-malicious package version",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Known-malicious package version"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A skill dependency, direct or transitive, at a version OSV lists as malicious: a compromised maintainer account, protestware, or a package that was malware from the start."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A skill dependency, direct or transitive, at a version OSV lists as malicious: a compromised maintainer account, protestware, or a package that was malware from the start.\\n\\nHow it's detected: Lockfile-resolved versions (or exact \`package.json\` pins when there is no lockfile) are matched against \`shared/maliciousDb.ts\`: advisories OSV classifies as malware (MAL- ids, CWE-506, or a summary naming malware or malicious code) for packages with a history of compromise, plus any loaded with \`--vuln-db\`. A range specifier without a lockfile only matches a package that is malicious in every version. npm dependencies only.\\n\\nRemediation: Remove the package or move to a version published before or after the compromise, delete node_modules and reinstall from a clean lockfile, and rotate any credentials available to the machine that ran it: malicious install scripts typically steal tokens and keys.",
                "markdown": "A skill dependency, direct or transitive, at a version OSV lists as malicious: a compromised maintainer account, protestware, or a package that was malware from the start.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** Lockfile-resolved versions (or exact \`package.json\` pins when there is no lockfile) are matched against \`shared/maliciousDb.ts\`: advisories OSV classifies as malware (MAL- ids, CWE-506, or a summary naming malware or malicious code) for packages with a history of compromise, plus any loaded with \`--vuln-db\`. A range specifier without a lockfile only matches a package that is malicious in every version. npm dependencies only.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Remove the package or move to a version published before or after the compromise, delete node_modules and reinstall from a clean lockfile, and rotate any credentials available to the machine that ran it: malicious install scripts typically steal tokens and keys."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-sup-007--known-malicious-package-version",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "supply-chain",
                "owasp": "LLM05: Supply Chain",
                "severity": "critical",
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "supply-chain"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-SUP-008",
              "name": "Project MCP servers approved without review",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Project MCP servers approved without review"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Claude Code configured to start every server in a project's \`.mcp.json\` without asking: any repository it's opened in can launch its own processes."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Claude Code configured to start every server in a project's \`.mcp.json\` without asking: any repository it's opened in can launch its own processes.\\n\\nHow it's detected: \`enableAllProjectMcpServers\` is \`true\` in any settings file. In user settings it applies to every repository you open, including ones you just cloned.\\n\\nRemediation: Remove \`enableAllProjectMcpServers\` and approve servers by name with \`enabledMcpjsonServers\`, after reviewing each one.",
                "markdown": "Claude Code configured to start every server in a project's \`.mcp.json\` without asking: any repository it's opened in can launch its own processes.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** \`enableAllProjectMcpServers\` is \`true\` in any settings file. In user settings it applies to every repository you open, including ones you just cloned.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Remove \`enableAllProjectMcpServers\` and approve servers by name with \`enabledMcpjsonServers\`, after reviewing each one."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-sup-008--project-mcp-servers-approved-without-review",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "supply-chain",
                "owasp": "LLM05: Supply Chain",
                "severity": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "supply-chain"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-SUP-009",
              "name": "Claude Code hook runs downloaded code",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Claude Code hook runs downloaded code"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A command Claude Code runs on its own (a hook, \`statusLine\`, \`fileSuggestion\`, or \`apiKeyHelper\`) that executes code fetched at run time, so whoever controls that URL controls your machine on every trigger."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A command Claude Code runs on its own (a hook, \`statusLine\`, \`fileSuggestion\`, or \`apiKeyHelper\`) that executes code fetched at run time, so whoever controls that URL controls your machine on every trigger.\\n\\nHow it's detected: The same patterns as CHAP-SUP-004: \`curl\`/\`wget\` piped into a shell, \`bash <(curl …)\`, \`iwr … | iex\`, \`base64 -d … | sh\`, \`sudo\`, and package installs.\\n\\nRemediation: Vendor the script into the repository (and review it) instead of piping a download into a shell, and avoid \`sudo\` in commands Claude Code runs automatically.",
                "markdown": "A command Claude Code runs on its own (a hook, \`statusLine\`, \`fileSuggestion\`, or \`apiKeyHelper\`) that executes code fetched at run time, so whoever controls that URL controls your machine on every trigger.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** The same patterns as CHAP-SUP-004: \`curl\`/\`wget\` piped into a shell, \`bash <(curl …)\`, \`iwr … | iex\`, \`base64 -d … | sh\`, \`sudo\`, and package installs.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Vendor the script into the repository (and review it) instead of piping a download into a shell, and avoid \`sudo\` in commands Claude Code runs automatically."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-sup-009--claude-code-hook-runs-downloaded-code",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "supply-chain",
                "owasp": "LLM05: Supply Chain",
                "severity": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "supply-chain"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-INJ-001",
              "name": "Untrusted input flows straight to the model",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Untrusted input flows straight to the model"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An active inbound message channel with no trust boundary separating untrusted content before it reaches the model."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An active inbound message channel with no trust boundary separating untrusted content before it reaches the model.\\n\\nHow it's detected: At least one \`channels.*.enabled\` is \`true\` in config, and \`trust.mark_untrusted_input\` is not \`true\`.\\n\\nRemediation: Mark untrusted inbound content explicitly, keep it separated from system instructions in the prompt, and filter it before forwarding to the model.",
                "markdown": "An active inbound message channel with no trust boundary separating untrusted content before it reaches the model.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** At least one \`channels.*.enabled\` is \`true\` in config, and \`trust.mark_untrusted_input\` is not \`true\`.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Mark untrusted inbound content explicitly, keep it separated from system instructions in the prompt, and filter it before forwarding to the model."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-inj-001--untrusted-input-flows-straight-to-the-model",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "injection",
                "owasp": "LLM01: Prompt Injection",
                "severity": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "injection"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-INJ-002",
              "name": "Tool output treated as trusted",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Tool output treated as trusted"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A skill whose output could drive another tool with no validation step — either a confirmed data-flow chain (a network/filesystem result traced into a shell-exec call's argument) or, more weakly, just both capabilities being present in the same file."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A skill whose output could drive another tool with no validation step — either a confirmed data-flow chain (a network/filesystem result traced into a shell-exec call's argument) or, more weakly, just both capabilities being present in the same file.\\n\\nHow it's detected: A skill that can execute shell commands and also has network or filesystem-read capability. When a bounded intra-file taint trace confirms the network/fs result actually reaches the shell-exec call's argument, this is reported at \`high\` severity as a confirmed chain; otherwise it's the weaker \`medium\`-severity shape-match (both capabilities merely present, not traced) — Chaperone still has no cross-function/file data-flow analysis.\\n\\nRemediation: Validate/escape a tool's output before it can drive another tool; never auto-execute model or tool output.",
                "markdown": "A skill whose output could drive another tool with no validation step — either a confirmed data-flow chain (a network/filesystem result traced into a shell-exec call's argument) or, more weakly, just both capabilities being present in the same file.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** A skill that can execute shell commands and also has network or filesystem-read capability. When a bounded intra-file taint trace confirms the network/fs result actually reaches the shell-exec call's argument, this is reported at \`high\` severity as a confirmed chain; otherwise it's the weaker \`medium\`-severity shape-match (both capabilities merely present, not traced) — Chaperone still has no cross-function/file data-flow analysis.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Validate/escape a tool's output before it can drive another tool; never auto-execute model or tool output."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-inj-002--tool-output-treated-as-trusted",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "injection",
                "owasp": "LLM02: Insecure Output Handling",
                "severity": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "injection"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-INJ-003",
              "name": "Actions triggerable by inbound messages without an allowlist",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Actions triggerable by inbound messages without an allowlist"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Any inbound message being able to invoke any tool."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Any inbound message being able to invoke any tool.\\n\\nHow it's detected: At least one channel is active and \`trust.tool_allowlist\` is empty or absent (same manifest-convention caveat as CHAP-AGY-003/004).\\n\\nRemediation: Restrict which tools each channel/sender can invoke with an explicit allowlist.",
                "markdown": "Any inbound message being able to invoke any tool.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** At least one channel is active and \`trust.tool_allowlist\` is empty or absent (same manifest-convention caveat as CHAP-AGY-003/004).\\n\\n**Remediation:** Restrict which tools each channel/sender can invoke with an explicit allowlist."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-inj-003--actions-triggerable-by-inbound-messages-without-an-allowlist",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "injection",
                "owasp": "LLM01: Prompt Injection / LLM08: Excessive Agency",
                "severity": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "injection"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-INJ-004",
              "name": "Auto-execution of links/commands from messages",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Auto-execution of links/commands from messages"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Config that auto-opens links or auto-runs commands found in inbound messages."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Config that auto-opens links or auto-runs commands found in inbound messages.\\n\\nHow it's detected: \`trust.auto_execute_links\` is \`true\`.\\n\\nRemediation: Disable auto-execution of links/commands found in messages; require explicit confirmation instead.",
                "markdown": "Config that auto-opens links or auto-runs commands found in inbound messages.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** \`trust.auto_execute_links\` is \`true\`.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Disable auto-execution of links/commands found in messages; require explicit confirmation instead."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-inj-004--auto-execution-of-linkscommands-from-messages",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "injection",
                "owasp": "LLM01: Prompt Injection",
                "severity": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "injection"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-INJ-005",
              "name": "Inbound channels do not distinguish trust level",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Inbound channels do not distinguish trust level"
