            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-SUP-002",
              "name": "No integrity verification for skill dependencies",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "No integrity verification for skill dependencies"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Skill dependencies installed with no lockfile."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Skill dependencies installed with no lockfile.\\n\\nHow it's detected: The skill declares at least one runtime dependency with no lockfile alongside it. npm: a \`package.json\` \`dependencies\` entry and no \`package-lock.json\`/\`yarn.lock\`/\`pnpm-lock.yaml\`. Python (when there is no \`package.json\`): a \`requirements.txt\` or \`pyproject.toml\` dependency and no \`poetry.lock\`/\`uv.lock\`/\`Pipfile.lock\`/\`pdm.lock\`; a \`requirements.txt\` whose every entry has a \`--hash=\` counts as its own lockfile. A manifest with no dependencies has nothing to install, so it is not flagged.\\n\\nRemediation: Commit a lockfile alongside the manifest and enable integrity checks.",
                "markdown": "Skill dependencies installed with no lockfile.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** The skill declares at least one runtime dependency with no lockfile alongside it. npm: a \`package.json\` \`dependencies\` entry and no \`package-lock.json\`/\`yarn.lock\`/\`pnpm-lock.yaml\`. Python (when there is no \`package.json\`): a \`requirements.txt\` or \`pyproject.toml\` dependency and no \`poetry.lock\`/\`uv.lock\`/\`Pipfile.lock\`/\`pdm.lock\`; a \`requirements.txt\` whose every entry has a \`--hash=\` counts as its own lockfile. A manifest with no dependencies has nothing to install, so it is not flagged.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Commit a lockfile alongside the manifest and enable integrity checks."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-sup-002--no-integrity-verification-for-skill-dependencies",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "supply-chain",
                "owasp": "LLM05: Supply Chain",
                "severity": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "supply-chain"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-SUP-003",
              "name": "Known-vulnerable dependency",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Known-vulnerable dependency"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A skill dependency, direct or transitive, whose version matches a known vulnerability in a bundled, offline OSV snapshot (or an OSV export passed with \`--vuln-db\`)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A skill dependency, direct or transitive, whose version matches a known vulnerability in a bundled, offline OSV snapshot (or an OSV export passed with \`--vuln-db\`).\\n\\nHow it's detected: When the skill has a \`package-lock.json\`, \`npm-shrinkwrap.json\`, \`yarn.lock\`, or \`pnpm-lock.yaml\`, every resolved package version in it, direct and transitive, is matched exactly. Without a lockfile, each \`package.json\` specifier's first X.Y.Z token stands in for the version, and the message says so; a specifier with no such token (\`\\"latest\\"\`, \`\\"*\\"\`, a git URL) is skipped. The bundled snapshot (\`shared/vulnDb.ts\`) covers about 80 packages agent skills commonly use and is refreshed out-of-band with \`npm run refresh:vulndb\`, never during a scan. Its date is shown in the report header. npm dependencies only.\\n\\nRemediation: Upgrade the dependency to a patched version (or remove it if unused). Run \`npm audit\` for a live check beyond the offline snapshot, or pass a full OSV export with \`--vuln-db\`.",
                "markdown": "A skill dependency, direct or transitive, whose version matches a known vulnerability in a bundled, offline OSV snapshot (or an OSV export passed with \`--vuln-db\`).\\n\\n**How it's detected:** When the skill has a \`package-lock.json\`, \`npm-shrinkwrap.json\`, \`yarn.lock\`, or \`pnpm-lock.yaml\`, every resolved package version in it, direct and transitive, is matched exactly. Without a lockfile, each \`package.json\` specifier's first X.Y.Z token stands in for the version, and the message says so; a specifier with no such token (\`\\"latest\\"\`, \`\\"*\\"\`, a git URL) is skipped. The bundled snapshot (\`shared/vulnDb.ts\`) covers about 80 packages agent skills commonly use and is refreshed out-of-band with \`npm run refresh:vulndb\`, never during a scan. Its date is shown in the report header. npm dependencies only.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Upgrade the dependency to a patched version (or remove it if unused). Run \`npm audit\` for a live check beyond the offline snapshot, or pass a full OSV export with \`--vuln-db\`."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-sup-003--known-vulnerable-dependency",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "supply-chain",
                "owasp": "LLM05: Supply Chain",
                "severity": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "supply-chain"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-SUP-004",
              "name": "Dangerous install pattern",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Dangerous install pattern"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Skill install scripts/docs that pipe a remote script into a shell, use \`sudo\`, or bootstrap a system package manager."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Skill install scripts/docs that pipe a remote script into a shell, use \`sudo\`, or bootstrap a system package manager.\\n\\nHow it's detected: A download executed by a shell: \`curl\`/\`wget\` piped into \`sh\`/\`bash\`/\`zsh\` (including \`| sudo bash\` and \`| bash -s\`), \`bash <(curl …)\`, PowerShell \`iwr\`/\`irm … | iex\`, or \`base64 -d … | sh\`; plus \`sudo\`. Searched in \`package.json\`'s \`preinstall\`/\`install\`/\`postinstall\`/\`prepare\` scripts (\`prepare\` runs on a git install), every \`*.sh\` file anywhere in the skill, and any top-level \`README*\`. \`apt-get install\`/\`brew install\` count only in install scripts and \`*.sh\` files, since a README recommending a package manager is normal.\\n\\nRemediation: Review the install script by hand; prefer a vetted, minimal setup with no piped-shell or sudo steps.",
                "markdown": "Skill install scripts/docs that pipe a remote script into a shell, use \`sudo\`, or bootstrap a system package manager.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** A download executed by a shell: \`curl\`/\`wget\` piped into \`sh\`/\`bash\`/\`zsh\` (including \`| sudo bash\` and \`| bash -s\`), \`bash <(curl …)\`, PowerShell \`iwr\`/\`irm … | iex\`, or \`base64 -d … | sh\`; plus \`sudo\`. Searched in \`package.json\`'s \`preinstall\`/\`install\`/\`postinstall\`/\`prepare\` scripts (\`prepare\` runs on a git install), every \`*.sh\` file anywhere in the skill, and any top-level \`README*\`. \`apt-get install\`/\`brew install\` count only in install scripts and \`*.sh\` files, since a README recommending a package manager is normal.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Review the install script by hand; prefer a vetted, minimal setup with no piped-shell or sudo steps."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-sup-004--dangerous-install-pattern",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "supply-chain",
                "owasp": "LLM05: Supply Chain",
                "severity": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "supply-chain"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-SUP-005",
              "name": "Obfuscated or dynamically-evaluated code",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Obfuscated or dynamically-evaluated code"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A skill using eval(), the Function constructor, or a decode-then-execute chain (e.g. eval(atob(payload))) to run dynamically-constructed code."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A skill using eval(), the Function constructor, or a decode-then-execute chain (e.g. eval(atob(payload))) to run dynamically-constructed code.\\n\\nHow it's detected: Any call to the \`eval\`/\`Function\` globals in a skill's source: bare, \`new Function(...)\`, indirect (\`(0, eval)(...)\`), through a global object (\`globalThis.eval\`, \`window.eval\`, \`self['eval']\`), or through a local alias. Also \`vm\` code execution (\`runInNewContext\`, \`runInThisContext\`, \`new vm.Script\`, \`compileFunction\`), \`require\`/\`import()\` of a non-literal module name, \`setTimeout\`/\`setInterval\` with a string, and \`module._compile\`. Files over 256 KB (typically minified bundles) aren't parsed; a pattern pre-pass looks for \`eval(\`, \`Function(\`, \`atob(\`, or \`child_process\` instead, and the report's Skipped section says which matched. Flagged unconditionally regardless of what's being evaluated — a real backdoor and a benign use are equally invisible to static review once code is constructed/evaluated at runtime, so there's no confident way to distinguish them from source alone.\\n\\nRemediation: Avoid dynamic code evaluation entirely. If genuinely needed, review the exact string being evaluated by hand and vendor/pin it rather than constructing or fetching it at runtime.",
                "markdown": "A skill using eval(), the Function constructor, or a decode-then-execute chain (e.g. eval(atob(payload))) to run dynamically-constructed code.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** Any call to the \`eval\`/\`Function\` globals in a skill's source: bare, \`new Function(...)\`, indirect (\`(0, eval)(...)\`), through a global object (\`globalThis.eval\`, \`window.eval\`, \`self['eval']\`), or through a local alias. Also \`vm\` code execution (\`runInNewContext\`, \`runInThisContext\`, \`new vm.Script\`, \`compileFunction\`), \`require\`/\`import()\` of a non-literal module name, \`setTimeout\`/\`setInterval\` with a string, and \`module._compile\`. Files over 256 KB (typically minified bundles) aren't parsed; a pattern pre-pass looks for \`eval(\`, \`Function(\`, \`atob(\`, or \`child_process\` instead, and the report's Skipped section says which matched. Flagged unconditionally regardless of what's being evaluated — a real backdoor and a benign use are equally invisible to static review once code is constructed/evaluated at runtime, so there's no confident way to distinguish them from source alone.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Avoid dynamic code evaluation entirely. If genuinely needed, review the exact string being evaluated by hand and vendor/pin it rather than constructing or fetching it at runtime."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-sup-005--obfuscated-or-dynamically-evaluated-code",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "supply-chain",
                "owasp": "LLM05: Supply Chain",
                "severity": "critical",
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "supply-chain"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-SUP-006",
              "name": "Typosquat-risk dependency name",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Typosquat-risk dependency name"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A skill dependency whose name is suspiciously close (small edit distance) to a well-known popular package name (e.g. 'reqeust' vs 'request') — a common typosquatting technique."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A skill dependency whose name is suspiciously close (small edit distance) to a well-known popular package name (e.g. 'reqeust' vs 'request') — a common typosquatting technique.\\n\\nHow it's detected: The dependency name isn't itself a well-known package, is at least 4 characters, and has a Levenshtein (edit) distance of 1-2 from a well-known package name in a small bundled reference list (no live registry lookup — Chaperone makes no outbound network calls). Not exhaustive: a name not close to anything on that list is never flagged.\\n\\nRemediation: Double check the exact spelling against the real package on the registry before installing, and remove the dependency if it was added by mistake.",
                "markdown": "A skill dependency whose name is suspiciously close (small edit distance) to a well-known popular package name (e.g. 'reqeust' vs 'request') — a common typosquatting technique.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** The dependency name isn't itself a well-known package, is at least 4 characters, and has a Levenshtein (edit) distance of 1-2 from a well-known package name in a small bundled reference list (no live registry lookup — Chaperone makes no outbound network calls). Not exhaustive: a name not close to anything on that list is never flagged.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Double check the exact spelling against the real package on the registry before installing, and remove the dependency if it was added by mistake."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-sup-006--typosquat-risk-dependency-name",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "supply-chain",
                "owasp": "LLM05: Supply Chain",
                "severity": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "supply-chain"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-SUP-007",
              "name": "Known-malicious package version",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Known-malicious package version"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A skill dependency, direct or transitive, at a version OSV lists as malicious: a compromised maintainer account, protestware, or a package that was malware from the start."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A skill dependency, direct or transitive, at a version OSV lists as malicious: a compromised maintainer account, protestware, or a package that was malware from the start.\\n\\nHow it's detected: Lockfile-resolved versions (or exact \`package.json\` pins when there is no lockfile) are matched against \`shared/maliciousDb.ts\`: advisories OSV classifies as malware (MAL- ids, CWE-506, or a summary naming malware or malicious code) for packages with a history of compromise, plus any loaded with \`--vuln-db\`. A range specifier without a lockfile only matches a package that is malicious in every version. npm dependencies only.\\n\\nRemediation: Remove the package or move to a version published before or after the compromise, delete node_modules and reinstall from a clean lockfile, and rotate any credentials available to the machine that ran it: malicious install scripts typically steal tokens and keys.",
                "markdown": "A skill dependency, direct or transitive, at a version OSV lists as malicious: a compromised maintainer account, protestware, or a package that was malware from the start.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** Lockfile-resolved versions (or exact \`package.json\` pins when there is no lockfile) are matched against \`shared/maliciousDb.ts\`: advisories OSV classifies as malware (MAL- ids, CWE-506, or a summary naming malware or malicious code) for packages with a history of compromise, plus any loaded with \`--vuln-db\`. A range specifier without a lockfile only matches a package that is malicious in every version. npm dependencies only.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Remove the package or move to a version published before or after the compromise, delete node_modules and reinstall from a clean lockfile, and rotate any credentials available to the machine that ran it: malicious install scripts typically steal tokens and keys."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-sup-007--known-malicious-package-version",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "supply-chain",
                "owasp": "LLM05: Supply Chain",
                "severity": "critical",
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "supply-chain"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-SUP-008",
              "name": "Project MCP servers approved without review",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Project MCP servers approved without review"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Claude Code configured to start every server in a project's \`.mcp.json\` without asking: any repository it's opened in can launch its own processes."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Claude Code configured to start every server in a project's \`.mcp.json\` without asking: any repository it's opened in can launch its own processes.\\n\\nHow it's detected: \`enableAllProjectMcpServers\` is \`true\` in any settings file. In user settings it applies to every repository you open, including ones you just cloned.\\n\\nRemediation: Remove \`enableAllProjectMcpServers\` and approve servers by name with \`enabledMcpjsonServers\`, after reviewing each one.",
                "markdown": "Claude Code configured to start every server in a project's \`.mcp.json\` without asking: any repository it's opened in can launch its own processes.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** \`enableAllProjectMcpServers\` is \`true\` in any settings file. In user settings it applies to every repository you open, including ones you just cloned.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Remove \`enableAllProjectMcpServers\` and approve servers by name with \`enabledMcpjsonServers\`, after reviewing each one."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-sup-008--project-mcp-servers-approved-without-review",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "supply-chain",
                "owasp": "LLM05: Supply Chain",
                "severity": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "supply-chain"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-SUP-009",
              "name": "Claude Code hook runs downloaded code",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Claude Code hook runs downloaded code"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A command Claude Code runs on its own (a hook, \`statusLine\`, \`fileSuggestion\`, or \`apiKeyHelper\`) that executes code fetched at run time, so whoever controls that URL controls your machine on every trigger."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A command Claude Code runs on its own (a hook, \`statusLine\`, \`fileSuggestion\`, or \`apiKeyHelper\`) that executes code fetched at run time, so whoever controls that URL controls your machine on every trigger.\\n\\nHow it's detected: The same patterns as CHAP-SUP-004: \`curl\`/\`wget\` piped into a shell, \`bash <(curl …)\`, \`iwr … | iex\`, \`base64 -d … | sh\`, \`sudo\`, and package installs.\\n\\nRemediation: Vendor the script into the repository (and review it) instead of piping a download into a shell, and avoid \`sudo\` in commands Claude Code runs automatically.",
                "markdown": "A command Claude Code runs on its own (a hook, \`statusLine\`, \`fileSuggestion\`, or \`apiKeyHelper\`) that executes code fetched at run time, so whoever controls that URL controls your machine on every trigger.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** The same patterns as CHAP-SUP-004: \`curl\`/\`wget\` piped into a shell, \`bash <(curl …)\`, \`iwr … | iex\`, \`base64 -d … | sh\`, \`sudo\`, and package installs.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Vendor the script into the repository (and review it) instead of piping a download into a shell, and avoid \`sudo\` in commands Claude Code runs automatically."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-sup-009--claude-code-hook-runs-downloaded-code",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "supply-chain",
                "owasp": "LLM05: Supply Chain",
                "severity": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "supply-chain"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-INJ-001",
              "name": "Untrusted input flows straight to the model",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Untrusted input flows straight to the model"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An active inbound message channel with no trust boundary separating untrusted content before it reaches the model."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An active inbound message channel with no trust boundary separating untrusted content before it reaches the model.\\n\\nHow it's detected: At least one \`channels.*.enabled\` is \`true\` in config, and \`trust.mark_untrusted_input\` is not \`true\`.\\n\\nRemediation: Mark untrusted inbound content explicitly, keep it separated from system instructions in the prompt, and filter it before forwarding to the model.",
                "markdown": "An active inbound message channel with no trust boundary separating untrusted content before it reaches the model.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** At least one \`channels.*.enabled\` is \`true\` in config, and \`trust.mark_untrusted_input\` is not \`true\`.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Mark untrusted inbound content explicitly, keep it separated from system instructions in the prompt, and filter it before forwarding to the model."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-inj-001--untrusted-input-flows-straight-to-the-model",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "injection",
                "owasp": "LLM01: Prompt Injection",
                "severity": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "injection"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-INJ-002",
              "name": "Tool output treated as trusted",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Tool output treated as trusted"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A skill whose output could drive another tool with no validation step — either a confirmed data-flow chain (a network/filesystem result traced into a shell-exec call's argument) or, more weakly, just both capabilities being present in the same file."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A skill whose output could drive another tool with no validation step — either a confirmed data-flow chain (a network/filesystem result traced into a shell-exec call's argument) or, more weakly, just both capabilities being present in the same file.\\n\\nHow it's detected: A skill that can execute shell commands and also has network or filesystem-read capability. When a bounded intra-file taint trace confirms the network/fs result actually reaches the shell-exec call's argument, this is reported at \`high\` severity as a confirmed chain; otherwise it's the weaker \`medium\`-severity shape-match (both capabilities merely present, not traced) — Chaperone still has no cross-function/file data-flow analysis.\\n\\nRemediation: Validate/escape a tool's output before it can drive another tool; never auto-execute model or tool output.",
                "markdown": "A skill whose output could drive another tool with no validation step — either a confirmed data-flow chain (a network/filesystem result traced into a shell-exec call's argument) or, more weakly, just both capabilities being present in the same file.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** A skill that can execute shell commands and also has network or filesystem-read capability. When a bounded intra-file taint trace confirms the network/fs result actually reaches the shell-exec call's argument, this is reported at \`high\` severity as a confirmed chain; otherwise it's the weaker \`medium\`-severity shape-match (both capabilities merely present, not traced) — Chaperone still has no cross-function/file data-flow analysis.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Validate/escape a tool's output before it can drive another tool; never auto-execute model or tool output."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-inj-002--tool-output-treated-as-trusted",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "injection",
                "owasp": "LLM02: Insecure Output Handling",
                "severity": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "injection"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-INJ-003",
              "name": "Actions triggerable by inbound messages without an allowlist",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Actions triggerable by inbound messages without an allowlist"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Any inbound message being able to invoke any tool."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Any inbound message being able to invoke any tool.\\n\\nHow it's detected: At least one channel is active and \`trust.tool_allowlist\` is empty or absent (same manifest-convention caveat as CHAP-AGY-003/004).\\n\\nRemediation: Restrict which tools each channel/sender can invoke with an explicit allowlist.",
                "markdown": "Any inbound message being able to invoke any tool.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** At least one channel is active and \`trust.tool_allowlist\` is empty or absent (same manifest-convention caveat as CHAP-AGY-003/004).\\n\\n**Remediation:** Restrict which tools each channel/sender can invoke with an explicit allowlist."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-inj-003--actions-triggerable-by-inbound-messages-without-an-allowlist",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "category": "injection",
                "owasp": "LLM01: Prompt Injection / LLM08: Excessive Agency",
                "severity": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "injection"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CHAP-INJ-004",
              "name": "Auto-execution of links/commands from messages",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Auto-execution of links/commands from messages"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Config that auto-opens links or auto-runs commands found in inbound messages."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Config that auto-opens links or auto-runs commands found in inbound messages.\\n\\nHow it's detected: \`trust.auto_execute_links\` is \`true\`.\\n\\nRemediation: Disable auto-execution of links/commands found in messages; require explicit confirmation instead.",
                "markdown": "Config that auto-opens links or auto-runs commands found in inbound messages.\\n\\n**How it's detected:** \`trust.auto_execute_links\` is \`true\`.\\n\\n**Remediation:** Disable auto-execution of links/commands found in messages; require explicit confirmation instead."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AlpayAltuntas/chaperone/blob/main/CHECKS.md#chap-inj-004--auto-execution-of-linkscommands-from-messages",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
